    "items": [
      {
        "text": "Richard Barrera is president of the San Diego Unified School Board and a senior policy adviser at the California Department of Education. He has served on the school board since 2008. According to his official biography, during his tenure the district passed four school bonds totaling $11.6 billion and ranked first in reading and second in math among large urban districts on the Nation's Report Card. His campaign says he led the expansion of transitional kindergarten to all four-year-olds, making San Diego the first California district to guarantee it. He says he pushed earlier this year to build over 2,000 units of affordable housing for teachers.",
        "cites": [1, 2, 3],
        "sentences": [
          {
            "text": "Richard Barrera is president of the San Diego Unified School Board and a senior policy adviser at the California Department of Education.",
            "cites": [1]
          },
          {
            "text": "He has served on the school board since 2008.",
            "cites": [1]
          },
          {
            "text": "According to his official biography, during his tenure the district passed four school bonds totaling $11.6 billion and ranked first in reading and second in math among large urban districts on the Nation's Report Card.",
            "cites": [1]
          },
          {
            "text": "His campaign says he led the expansion of transitional kindergarten to all four-year-olds, making San Diego the first California district to guarantee it.",
            "cites": [2]
          },
          {
            "text": "He says he pushed earlier this year to build over 2,000 units of affordable housing for teachers.",
            "cites": [3]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "He has pledged to treat Proposition 98 as a floor, not a ceiling, and to secure stable school funding. He criticized the governor's decision to withhold some Proposition 98 funding. He supports better pay, benefits, training pipelines and affordable housing for educators. He opposes making SAT or ACT scores a UC admissions requirement, arguing that universities should teach incoming students foundational math if needed. He promises to enforce civil rights protections for LGBTQ+ and immigrant students.",
        "cites": [4, 5, 6, 7],
